# Networks that traffic is likely spoofed if received on the external
# interface.
#   This traffic is dropped, unless the external interface is in that network.
#
# Source: IANA IPv6 Special-Purpose Address Registry, last updated 2025-10-09
#   https://www.iana.org/assignments/iana-ipv6-special-registry/iana-ipv6-special-registry.xhtml
#
# Selected on that registry's own "Globally Reachable" column: a block marked False has no
# business arriving from the internet, so a packet claiming one as its source on the external
# device is forged. Blocks marked True are omitted whatever else they are.
#
# TWO ENTRIES ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and both would break IPv6 if added.
#
#   fe80::/10  Link-Local Unicast. The registry marks it not globally reachable and it is
#              still the ordinary source address for neighbour discovery and router
#              advertisements, which arrive on the external device by definition. Drop it
#              and the host stops resolving its neighbours. This is where IPv6 parts company
#              with the IPv4 list beside it: ARP is layer 2 and never carries an IP source,
#              so 169.254.0.0/16 is safe to drop there and fe80::/10 is not safe here.
#
#   ::/128     Unspecified. It is the required source for duplicate address detection, which
#              a host on the same link performs before it has an address to send from.
#
# ff00::/8 is not in the registry - it is the multicast allocation - and is listed anyway,
# because a multicast address is never a valid source. It is the counterpart of 224.0.0.0/4
# in the IPv4 list.
::1/128
::ffff:0:0/96
64:ff9b:1::/48
100::/64
100:0:0:1::/64
2001:2::/48
2001:10::/28
2001:db8::/32
3fff::/20
5f00::/16
fc00::/7
ff00::/8
